  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 1 card dq reg: grade 6.7: "How can the periodic table predict the kind of bond two elements form?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 3 s03-dq: grade 6.7: "How can the periodic table predict the kind of bond two elements form?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 6 s06-rule: grade 6.7: "A metal atom loses its valence electrons and becomes a + ion (cation). A nonmetal atom gains them and becomes a − ion (anion). The transfer alone costs energy."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 7 cr-rule: grade 6: "The pull (attraction) between + ions and − ions is the ionic bond. The formula NaCl gives a ratio, not a molecule."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 8 s08-rule: grade 6.6: "A pair of electrons shared by two atoms is a covalent bond. Atoms joined this way form a molecule (molécula)."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 9 s09-rule: grade 7.2: "The electrons spend more time near one atom. It is the atom with the higher electronegativity. That atom gets a small negative charge, written δ− (delta minus). The other atom gets a small positive charge, δ+."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 10 s10-rule: grade 6.6: "Metal cations sit in a sea of delocalized electrons. Their attraction is the metallic bond. Real electrons behave as waves, not dots."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 13 card prompt reg: grade 6.7: "Write one sentence that compares the particles and attractions in three kinds of bonds."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 13 s13-s: grade 7.1: "Ionic bonds hold oppositely charged ions. Covalent bonds share pairs of electrons. Metallic bonds hold cations in an electron sea."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 15 s15-rule: grade 5.4: "Subtract the smaller value from the larger one. Below 0.4, the bond is nonpolar covalent. From 0.4 to 1.8, it is polar covalent. Above 1.8, it is ionic."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 16 s16-rule: grade 7.9: "A metal and a nonmetal: electrons transfer. Ionic bond. Two nonmetals: electrons are shared. Covalent bond. A metal alone: electrons are delocalized, a sea. Metallic bond."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 21 card prompt reg: grade 5.4: "A student calls the transferred electron the ionic bond. Explain the error. What holds solid salt together?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 21 s21-sample: grade 6.7: "The transfer only forms the ions. The attraction between oppositely charged ions is the ionic bond."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 22 card prompt reg: grade 5.1: "Cl 2 and HCl each contain one shared electron pair. Explain why Cl 2 is nonpolar, but HCl is polar. Identify the δ− atom."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 22 s22-sample: grade 6.5: "Both Cl atoms have the same electronegativity. In HCl, Cl pulls harder and is δ−."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 23 card prompt reg: grade 7.1: "A copper wire carries an electric current. Use the electron-sea model to explain why. Name one limitation of the model."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 23 s23-sample: grade 7.8: "Delocalized electrons flow through the metal, so copper conducts. Real electrons are waves."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 24 card prompt reg: grade 6.7: "How can the periodic table predict the kind of bond two elements form?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 24 s24-sample: grade 5.1: "Look up both values on the table and subtract. A metal and a nonmetal above 1.8 form ions. Two nonmetals share electrons."
